<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-euros">
  <title>Home or away: where do soccer players play?</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>"Milan or Madrid, as long as it's Italy."</p>
      <p>--- Andy Moller, then of Dortmund</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      The European Football Championship has been held every four years since 1960 and has grown substantially in
      size and influence.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Questions.</em> Do players represent national teams while playing club football in their own country?
      How has this changed over time?
    </p>
    <p><em>Sources.</em> EURO player dataset assembled by Abel.</p>
    <p><em>Structure.</em> 4,012 players and 21 variables.</p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-euros-history">
    <title>The European Football Championship since 1960</title>

    <p>
      The finals expanded from 4 teams (1960) to 8 (1980), 16 (1996), and 24 (2016 onward). Squad sizes also grew
      from 17 up to 26 players.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-uefatq">
      <image source="images/uefaTQ-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Numbers of teams (top) and squad sizes (bottom) over the 16 European Championships. Point area in the
          lower plot is proportional to the number of countries with that value.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Early variation likely reflects looser squad regulations. Later tournaments mostly follow fixed limits, with
      a few notable exceptions.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-uefaperc">
      <image source="images/uefaPerc-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Percentage of each nation's squad playing in that nation's own league across the 16 championships, with a
          loess smooth and point areas proportional to country counts.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The long-term trend is downward: over time, more players represent national teams while playing in foreign
      leagues.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-euros-leagues">
    <title>Which leagues do international players play in?</title>

    <p>
      England, Germany, Italy, and Spain dominate as destinations for EURO players, with England becoming more
      prominent in recent tournaments.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-uefanos">
      <image source="images/uefaNos-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Numbers of players from four major leagues.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      For EURO 2020, bar charts reveal where players from all squads were based at club level.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-uefa20l">
      <image source="images/uefa20L-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Numbers of EURO 2020 players playing in different European leagues.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-uefamap">
      <image source="images/uefaMap.png"/>
      <caption><p>Numbers of EURO 2020 players playing in various European national leagues.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      A few players were based outside Europe. Within Europe, player distribution across leagues is highly uneven.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-uefa20f">
      <image source="images/uefa20f-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Numbers of Finland's EURO 2020 players by country of league.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-uefa20">
      <image source="images/uefa20-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Numbers of EURO 2020 players of each team by country of league.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Every team had players from multiple league countries. Some squads were highly concentrated in one domestic
      league; others were widely distributed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-euros-change">
    <title>How have countries changed?</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Long-term comparison is complicated by geopolitical changes, including country splits and reunifications,
        plus the UK home nations competing separately.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-uefapercm">
        <image source="images/uefaPercM-1.png"/>
        <caption>
          <p>
            Percentages of home-league players for countries appearing most often in the 16 championship finals.
          </p>
        </caption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Patterns vary by country: some show gradual decline, some sharp transitions in specific years, and others a
        return to high home-league shares.
      </p>

      <p>
        <em>Answers.</em> Increasingly, international players play abroad. At EURO 2020, just over half of all
        players were based in England, Germany, or Italy.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Further questions.</em> Which team has the largest long-run variability in home-player share? Do World
        Cup squads show similar patterns? Are club-squad nationality compositions available consistently?
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-euros-takeaways">
      <title>Graphical takeaways</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Aligned axes improve readability in ensembles of related graphics.</p></li>
        <li><p>Smooths can be informative even for discrete-valued datasets.</p></li>
        <li><p>Faceting scales well when comparing many groups.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
